## Real-World Example

Consider an E-Commerce Application:

### Components

* OrderService → Processes customer orders
* PaymentService → Handles payment transactions
* InventoryService → Manages stock availability
* NotificationService → Sends emails and alerts

Without Spring, each service would manually create and manage its dependencies.

With Spring:

* Dependencies are injected automatically.
* Components remain loosely coupled.
* Testing becomes easier.
* Code becomes more maintainable.

This allows developers to focus on business logic instead of object management.

